module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_constant_enforces_a_law_narrower_than_its_own_annotation

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data a_constant_enforces_a_law_narrower_than_its_own_annotation: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_constant_enforces_a_law_narrower_than_its_own_annotation" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "A SCALAR STANDS WHERE A RELATION BELONGS, so the law it enforces is the one its TYPE can express rather than the one its annotation states -- and the two differ by exactly the discriminant the scalar cannot carry. The invalid state is a guard that is simultaneously correct where it was measured and over-applied everywhere else, with nothing in the program able to tell the two populations apart.",
    "THE INSTANCE, 2026-09-11: gunbc.harness.harness_seat harness_interactive_quality_seats. Its annotation read 'one COLD-RISK turn in flight per serving group', following a measured hazard whose subject was specific -- a cold prefill landing beside a decode SOMEBODY IS READING collapses that decoder's inter-token latency. What a ceiling of one on a single pool enforces is 'one turn in flight per serving group', because the pool has no notion of who is reading. Every harness turn -- a worker assembling a tool call, a reviewer writing a verdict file, a probe printing an outcome at the end -- was therefore held to a law written for a reader, and a healthy route sat idle while their work queued.",
    "WHY IT SURVIVED: the annotation was ACCURATE about the measurement and the constant was ACCURATE about the measured case. Nothing was wrong at the point of declaration; the error is that the discriminant was carried in prose, where no Accepted program can read it (DESIGN section 4c), while the enforcement was carried in a number. So the over-application produced no refusal, no diagnostic and no counter -- only work that did not happen, which is the cheapest kind of harm to miss.",
    "THE REVIEW TELL, AND IT IS MECHANICAL: an annotation over a scalar that names a QUALIFIED subject -- a cold-risk turn, an interactive request, a first-party caller, a large input -- while the scalar's consumers can only count. Whenever the qualifier is real, the qualifier is a type and the number is per case of it. The repair is not a bigger number: it is the relation the annotation already described, with the scalar surviving as one case's ceiling.",
    "THE NEIGHBOURING CLASS THIS IS NOT, stated because the two will be confused. A guard that is correct and GUARDS THE WRONG OBJECT is a different failure: there the guard's own statement is right and it is pointed at the wrong subject. Here the guard is pointed at exactly the right subject and its STATEMENT is wider than the one its author wrote down, because the carrier -- a scalar -- cannot express the qualifier the annotation used. The two are distinguished by where the gap sits: between guard and object, or between guard and its own prose. That neighbouring row is being filed by another lane and is NOT cited here by module name while it is unlanded, because a citation whose target does not resolve is indistinguishable at the citing end from a typo; the citation is owed in both directions once it lands.",
    "THE SECOND-ORDER TELL, worth stating because it is what makes this class expensive rather than merely untidy: a number under-applied is loud (something refuses that should not), a number over-applied is silent (something never runs that could have). So this class is systematically discovered late, by someone asking why a resource is idle rather than by anything failing.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
